# component-dev.pkg -- what is needed to COMPILE AGAINST a component.
#
# -dev AND -src ARE NOT THE SAME PACKAGE, and the difference is the reason this
# kind exists rather than being folded into the other.  -src is what you need to
# REBUILD the component and is where its licence obligations are discharged;
# -dev is what you need to build something ELSE that talks to it -- the headers
# that declare its interface and the library that implements the client half.
# Somebody writing an MGR client wants mgr.h and the client library and has no
# use for the server's 1,953 files; somebody exercising the GPL wants all 1,953
# and has no use for a convenience library.
#
# TWO COMPONENTS DECLARE `dev', and each is one line per file or directory at
# the head of its own list -- `dev <path it installs at> <path in a checkout>',
# which is a directive and not an entry because none of it goes on an image:
#
#   net-dev  the socket interface -- inet_ipc.h, which is the request and reply
#            framing of the FIFO channel to /etc/inet and so the interface in
#            the strictest sense; inet_chan.h, netdb.h, arpa/inet.h and net/,
#            which declare what the calls take and return; and libsocket.a,
#            which resolves them.  The daemon's own internals (minix/,
#            netdb_priv.h) are not in it: a header a client must not include is
#            not part of an interface.
#   mgr-dev  MGR's client half -- include/mgr, the headers a client writes
#            against, include/c900, which they include, and libmgrcl.a, the
#            client library MGR's own build keeps apart from the server's.
#
# The producer resolves and packs them, as it does every other kind, because it
# is the repository the headers and the built library are in.  The same shape is
# what the TOOLCHAIN lane's headers-and-libraries packages take: include.pkg and
# libc.pkg are -dev packages of a component called `toolchain', written before
# the concept had a name, and they are not renamed onto this declaration --
# they carry a source id (tcid) a component package has no way to state.
kind      component-dev
what      one component's headers and link-time libraries: the compile-time interface
producer  dist/pack-component.sh
archive   c900-@C@-dev-v@V@.tar.gz
toplevel  c900-@C@-dev-v@V@
version   VERSION
stamp     .provenance  kind commit version package component pkgkind entries contentid
text      .provenance  ^package=@C@-dev$
text      .provenance  ^pkgkind=dev$
text      .provenance  ^component=@C@$
file      LICENSE
# ---- THE TERMS THE CONTENTS ARE UNDER ----
# LICENSE above is what this repository says about its OWN files.  What the
# component's CONTENTS are under travels with the release, not inside each
# archive: the texts are collected once into `licences' beside the packages,
# with an index.tab saying which regimes govern which component.  A source
# package carries each program's own COPYING beside the code as well, because
# it ships the source root upstream put it in.
file      manifest.tab
dir       files
# An interface is headers, or a library, or both -- but a -dev package with
# NEITHER is an empty promise, and `glob' with a floor of 1 over the union is
# what refuses it.  The floor is on files/, not on include/, so a component
# publishing only a library is not forced to invent a header.
glob      files/*  1
text      manifest.tab  ^f .
# ---- WHICH BYTES ----
# .contents is md5sum's own format over every other file here, and
# .provenance:contentid is its sha1, so a flipped byte is caught and named.
# Any consumer verifies it directly with `md5sum -c .contents'.
file      .contents
idfile    .contents  .provenance  contentid
